Z||000005||Master_Sushi||10-05-2001||09:25 PM||||Man that sucks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, wonder if the person that did that lived? It dosen't look like the car jsut fliped, it probably went off a hill/cliff or something. Looks like the top of the car is completely flat.<P>I've been under my car allot but never noticed how flat and clean the bottom of fieros are. Very smooth.<P>Also looks like the ppl are about to lift the car by the gas tank??? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> I don't see how that would work.||||reg||11
Z||000006||tomanyfieros||10-05-2001||09:38 PM||||i have an88 coupe that was rolled over twice. hit a ditch w/ balled tires n snow, that was the worst combination..... i would have put the fiero away, i bought it for $200, 5 spd, L4, parts car..... roof was barely pushed over, but it was over enough to make a windshield not fit in.... Jason<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dreamer.gif">    <BR><B>We all have dreams, but not everyone gets them...</B>||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||10-06-2001||03:51 AM||||Ok, NO PRODUCTION CAR may rool over under any circumstances on a *FLAT* asphalt skidpad (they specify coefficent of friction, it's basically a normal new grippy road on a sunny day) with stock wheels and tyres (and pressures, and fresh suspension, etc)<P>Please note that on the road, surfaces are almost NEVER flat, and conditions vary hugely. that said, the Fiero's CG is I belive below the axel line, all butpreventing flip in any type of tyres (even insanely grippy aftermarket) ON A FLAT SURFACE...<P>thorw in banking, bumps, etc and all bets are off.  Cars do not make good airplanes...<P>This is a tiny part of the huge process involved in getting an automobile certified for sales in the USA.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000008||DKOV||10-06-2001||04:20 AM||||Last season I raced my 88GT at PIR in the SPO classes and the car was really great fun and VERY competitive with just a few minor mods... Well, until it blew the driver's front strut that is!<P>It happened under braking from at around 110 mph or so into a hard double apex right.  The front end dropped while the inside rear tire lifted putting me into a very fast spin.  <P>The car DID lift as it slid sideways on the driver's side tires!  So bad that I was sure it was going to flip.  So I turned the wheels AWAY from the skid to rotate the car backwards, hoping to roll off the end of the track into the grass but I was still carrying to much speed and that little manouver, coupled with the fact I had NO front end suspension, spun the car around AGAIN!<P>This time, I could see the road and into the corner so I stomped the throttle and pointed the wheels at the apex and SOMEHOW drove the car out of the spin!  Luck!<P>Only when I stepped on the brakes again to catch my breath, the front end dipped again and I KNEW that something BAD had happened...<P>That was the end of my race day, but...<P>I know for a fact that you can indeed flat slide a 1988 Fiero GT at over 100mph without it flipping over  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Of course, who knows what would have happened if I had carried that slide into the grass where the sidewalls could find some bite  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I really don't recommend finding out!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000009||Archie||10-06-2001||08:17 AM||||Yes, I think you'd have to "trip" the car to get it to flip.<P>Let me tell you how I did it.<P>My first V-8 Fiero was a slightly-built 327 installed into an '84 Se with the 4-speed.   I had previously built the engine for my '67 Camaro, but later pulled it out in favor of a Big Block.   This 327 would run 6500 RPM all day long & several times I had peaked it at 7000 RPM, so suffice to say it was a screamer.<P>I used to be real active in TSD rallies.  For those that don't know, these are Time, Speed Distance Rallies, these events took place on<BR>public mostly country roads.   The concept of a TSD Rally is that you should not have the reason to speed or be other wise dangerous to<BR>everybody else who is out driving around on back roads on any given Sunday afternoon.  (but that's not much fun that way)  In those days we used to have these rallies on every old dirt country road the Rally Master could<BR>find, that was back in the days BWO, "Before the rallymasters Wimped Out", and decided that most of the club members didn't want to get their cars dusty. (bummer)<P>It was the spring of 1987, a real nice sunny Sunday afternoon, it had rained a lot the night before and I would say the dirt roads were damp, tightly packed and very fast. (sounds like a horse race don't it?) <BR>There were some puddles on the inside of many of the turns.   In our part of the country the old country roads wander in and out, left and right.   Many of these roads date back to the old horse and wagon days and have been cut down into the turf to the point that these old dirt roads would have what you could call a "curb" that was made of the sod<BR>of the adjoining farmland.<P>I, of course, was driving and my navigator was Larry (last name withheald upon request).  We were a pretty good team & had won more than our share of events, usually our regular competitors, upon seeing that we were at an event, would readily admit that they were competing for second place.   The Rally started out normally enough and before long we were on the dirt backroads of Berrien County Michigan.   Larry & I somehow managed to get lost very badly early on.   (I think it was his fault)  Upon realizing that we were out there, where ever we were, all<BR>alone we scanned down the instructions for a clue as to where we should be and where we might pick up the course again.   We found a familiar location that was about 15 miles Diagonally across the County from where<BR>we were.<P>As I said before, a TSD rally is not meant to be a speed event, however when you find yourself 15 miles from where you should be, the "Time" in TSD takes on a whole new meaning.   We set off for our destination and<BR>as we were headed Diagonally across the County, you can assume we were to be negotiating a number of turns on route.   Now I need to mention that I was very familliar with the handling of this car and was well<BR>aware what it took to take turns quickly on the dirt.   As you can imagine we were racing across the county at speeds approaching 100MPH on tight narrow dirt roads.   Anyone who is familliar with this part of the country will tell you that on country roads, it is not very often that you find a road that goes more than 2 miles before abruptly turning right or left for no apparent reason.   Approaching everyone of these 90 degree turns there is a diamond shaped sign with an arrow pointing the way and one of those little yellow turn speed signs right under it<BR>("15", "20" or whatever).   I was getting a pretty good routine going.  I was hitting 100+ on the straights (although I didn't look down much) and was braking hard down to maybe 50-60 at the start of the turn, I would then drop into 2nd and nail it thru the turn. That would 4-wheel drift me out to the outside "curb" as I accelerated away.  (what a kick!!!)  Larry, who would normally be quiet during this type of session, must have been getting a bit apprehensive.   As we approached the turns he started telling me what direction the arrow was pointed and<BR>what the speed sign said.  But we are still flying.   Larry kept doing that and each time I'm thinking to myself  JC Larry, I can see the F---ing sign. On one turn in particular, I remember it well (I could take you there now), While I'm thinking to myself "JC Larry, I can see the F---ing sign" I forgot to downshift and am very late braking.   OH<BR>OH the turn is starting & I'm still in 4th at about 90....ooooops.   Not a word said (aloud anyway) I slam 2nd gear yank the wheel to the right and try to power thru it, I slide out and both left tires hit the "curb".....flip bounce on the roof and land on the wheels about 50 feet out in the field.   Engine still running, look at Larry, he's covered in<BR>mud and there is a puddle on his seat, nail the throttle and continue like nothing happened a but little slower now.<P>That was our last Rally together, Larry mumbled something about teaching<BR>afternoon Sunday school Bible classes.   Never saw him again, and never could find a replacement navigator who could keep me winning like Larry could.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000010||88CoupeV6||10-06-2001||12:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DKOV:<BR><B>Well, until it blew the driver's front strut that is!<P>DKOV -</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't know what you broke but, Fieros don't have any struts in the front. The only thing that would most likely break is a ball joint.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Kasdan||10-06-2001||01:33 PM||||No front struts?  uhh...yeah they do...||||reg||1
